>
  <h2 id="what-is-mkv">What is MKV?</h2>
  <p>MKV (Matroska Video) is a highly flexible, open-source container format. Unlike MP4, which has strict limitations on the types of video and audio it can hold, MKV can encapsulate virtually any codec.</p>
  <p>It is especially popular among enthusiasts because it can hold unlimited video, audio, and subtitle tracks in a single file, making it the format of choice for ripping DVDs and Blu-rays.</p>

  <h2 id="comparison">Feature Comparison</h2>
  
  <ComparisonTable 
    caption="MP4 vs MKV Feature Comparison"
    headers={['Feature', 'MP4', 'MKV']}
    rows={[
      ['Subtitle Support', 'Limited (hardcoded or specific formats)', 'Unlimited (soft subs, multiple languages)'],
      ['Audio Tracks', 'Usually 1-2 (stereo/5.1)', 'Unlimited (multiple languages/commentaries)'],
      ['Compatibility', 'Universal', 'Requires specific players (VLC) or modern TVs'],
      ['Licensing', 'Patented', 'Open-source']
    ]}
  />

  <h2 id="which-to-choose">Which Should You Choose?</h2>
  <p>Choose <strong>MP4</strong> for maximum compatibility across all devices, mobile phones, and web browsers. Choose <strong>MKV</strong> if you need multiple audio tracks, high-quality soft subtitles, or are archiving high-definition content for playback on a dedicated media center.</p>
